  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch05.s01",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch05.s01.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p01.r01",
            "text": "Why bring in pretrained GPT-2 weights at all, instead of continuing with the model trained from scratch?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p01.r02",
            "text": "The text generated by the earlier training loop was not very coherent and was not making much sense.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@93",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@99",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@105",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@107",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@110"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p01.r03",
            "text": "That result is tied to the setup used there: the model had been trained on one small book, described as a very small data set, and run for a limited number of epochs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@113",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@115",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@118",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@120"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p01.r04",
            "text": "Even after temperature scaling and top-k sampling were introduced to reduce overfitting, the generated next sentence still did not really make too much sense.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@123",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@125",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@128",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@131",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@133",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@136",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@138",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@141",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@142",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@144"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p01.r05",
            "text": "A later comparison makes the same point clearly: without GPT-2 weights, the model produced output that did not make any sense at all.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2643",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2646",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2648",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2650",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2652"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s01.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p02.r01",
            "text": "The model uses pretrained OpenAI weights loaded into the GPT model so that text generation is coherent.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@15",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@17",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@21",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@23",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@26",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@28"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p02.r02",
            "text": "The exact GPT architecture built earlier is reused, and the same GPT model class is integrated with the GPT-2 OpenAI weights that were publicly released.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@37",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@40",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@45",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@47",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@50",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@52",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@56"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p02.r03",
            "text": "For example, without GPT-2 weights, the output did not make sense, and after loading the weights, the generated text is coherent enough that the words make sense.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2643",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2646",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2648",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2650",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2652",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2933",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2936",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2937",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2939"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p02.r04",
            "text": "There is one practical prerequisite gap to keep in mind when loading those released files.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p02.r05",
            "text": "OpenAI originally saved the GPT-2 weights with TensorFlow, whereas the code used here is in PyTorch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@260",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@262",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@265",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@267",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@269"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s01.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r01",
            "text": "Work with a language model does not end at pre-training, because after obtaining a pretrained model you can use the pretrained weights for specific applications by fine-tuning them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2947",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2948",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2949",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2952",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2955",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2963",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2965",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2967",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2968"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r02",
            "text": "Examples of such application-specific work include building a text classifier or an educational quiz app.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2955",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2956",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2959",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2961"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r03",
            "text": "The same GPT model built from scratch is kept for those application-oriented projects rather than switching to some other GPT model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2973",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2975",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2976",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2978",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2980",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2982"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r04",
            "text": "Fine-tuning means adapting a pretrained model to a specific task by training the model on additional data.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@12",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@16",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@19",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@21",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@24",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@27"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r05",
            "text": "In the usage emphasized here, fine-tuning starts from pretrained GPT-2 weights and then trains the model again on a specific data set so that it performs well on that data set.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2759",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2761",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2764",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2900",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2902",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2904",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2906"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p03.r06",
            "text": "Two kinds of fine-tuning are distinguished here: instruction fine-tuning and classification fine-tuning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@31",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@32",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@34",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@37",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@39",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2910",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2911",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2914",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2916",
              "0PpxZ3kNPWo@2918"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s01.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p04.r01",
            "text": "Now consider a hands-on project based on classification fine-tuning.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p04.r02",
            "text": "The classification fine-tuning project uses a large language model for email classification.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@43",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@46",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@48",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@51",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@55",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@57",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@60"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p04.r03",
            "text": "The specific task is to look at emails and classify them into one of two categories: spam or not spam.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@63",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@64",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@67",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@70"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s01.p04.r04",
            "text": "In that setup, the model takes the input text and the desired output is whether the email is spam or not spam.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@192",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@193",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@195",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@198",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@200",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@204",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@207",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@210",
              "f6zqClXOh7Y@212"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
